Add a logging middleware in app.js after `sessionUser` (so you have `req.user`) but before the route handlers. It would capture:

- Request: method, path, user ID, timestamp
- Response: status code, response time

Express middleware pattern:

```javascript
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const start = Date.now();
  res.on('finish', () => {
    const duration = Date.now() - start;
    console.log(`${req.method} ${req.path} ${res.statusCode} ${duration}ms user=${req.user?.id}`);
  });
  next();
});
```

This runs after sessionUser, so `req.user` exists. It listens for the response to finish so you capture the actual status code and timing.

For more detail (request body, database queries), you could also wrap the db functions or add logging inside route handlers—but middleware handles the cross-cutting concern cleanly in one place.

